content.theory:
**FizzBuzz** is the classic interview warm-up — a perfect project to combine loops and conditionals.

Rules: print the numbers 1 to 20, but:

- If a number is divisible by 3, print `Fizz`
- If divisible by 5, print `Buzz`
- If divisible by **both** 3 and 5, print `FizzBuzz`
- Otherwise print the number

The modulo operator `%` gives the remainder of a division: `10 % 3` is `1`. A number is divisible by 3 when `n % 3 == 0`.

The **order of checks matters** — check the both-case first:

```java
if (n % 15 == 0) {          // divisible by 3 AND 5
    System.out.println("FizzBuzz");
} else if (n % 3 == 0) {
    System.out.println("Fizz");
} else if (n % 5 == 0) {
    System.out.println("Buzz");
} else {
    System.out.println(n);
}
```

Why check `% 15` first? If you check `% 3` first, a number like 15 (divisible by both) would print `Fizz` and never reach the `FizzBuzz` branch.

codeSnippets.code:
for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
    if (i % 15 == 0) {
        System.out.println("FizzBuzz");
    } else if (i % 3 == 0) {
        System.out.println("Fizz");
    } else if (i % 5 == 0) {
        System.out.println("Buzz");
    } else {
        System.out.println(i);
    }
}

